      "program_name": "Streamlined ministerial approvals for affordable, supportive and farmworker housing (SB 35, AB 2162, AB 1783, SB 330) and Prohousing Designation",
      "administering_agency": "Ventura County Resource Management Agency, Planning Division",
      "funding_type": "zoning incentive",
      "typical_award_size": "Because the County has not met its above-moderate RHNA progress, qualifying multifamily projects with the required affordability are approved ministerially under SB 35 using the County's Streamlined Multifamily Zoning Clearance application; supportive housing is by right where multifamily is allowed (AB 2162); farmworker housing on agricultural land qualifies under AB 1783 and the County's Agricultural Worker Housing regulations (Somis Ranch farmworker complex, AMCAL). The County applied for the state Prohousing Designation in 2026 (public comment through May 29, 2026; Board resolution April 28, 2026), which adds points in state funding programs, and is completing a countywide Farmworker Housing Study and Action Plan (approved September 22, 2026).",
      "per_unit_cap": "Not applicable.",
      "affordability_requirement": "SB 35: 10% or 50% affordable depending on RHNA shortfall category; AB 2162 supportive housing thresholds; AB 1783 farmworker housing requirements.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "supportive housing",
        "mixed-income",
        "farmworker housing"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "rolling",
      "next_deadline": "Submit the Streamlined Multifamily Zoning Clearance application with objective standards compliance; SB 330 preliminary applications lock in standards.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "true",
      "layering_notes": "Ventura County's housing money runs through the County Executive Office, Community Development (CEO-CD, 800 S. Victoria Avenue, Ventura, (805) 654-2681), which administers the Urban County CDBG and HOME programs, the HOME Consortium, PLHA, General Fund housing NOFAs and Homekey sponsorships; the Resource Management Agency Planning Division handles density bonus, RHD zoning and streamlining; the Health Care Agency Behavioral Health holds BHSA; the Ventura County Continuum of Care (CA-611) sits in the CEO; and the Area Housing Authority of the County of Ventura (AHACV) supplies vouchers outside Oxnard, Ventura, Port Hueneme and Santa Paula, which have their own housing authorities. The nonprofit Housing Trust Fund Ventura County (HTFVC) is the de facto county revolving loan fund. The County has no inclusionary ordinance, linkage fee or dedicated housing tax. venturacounty.gov department pages block automated access, so records were built from vcportal.venturacounty.gov PDFs (Action Plan, PLHA plan, funding summaries), rma.venturacounty.gov, housingtrustfundvc.org, ahacv.org, HCD tables and press coverage. No County fee waiver or deferral for affordable housing was found; the County's incentives are procedural (ministerial approval, no CEQA) and locational (RHD sites, farmworker housing on agricultural land). SB 35 projects of 10 or more units require prevailing wage. Source is the RMA Streamlined Affordable Housing Development page.",
